{
  "addenda": [
    {
      "cause": "the host wrote events, and the runners wrote receipts, with paths exactly as the harness reported them. For the Claude runs that meant the full filesystem path of every installed plugin, from `system/init`, in every receipt from R2 onward \u2014 which discloses the owner home directory and the plugins installed in it. This repository is public.",
      "date": "2026-09-21",
      "fixed_in": "redaction at the recording boundary: `pio_core::redact_home` is applied in `Lifecycle::event` and `Lifecycle::complete`, and in the live runners before a receipt is written, so no absolute home path reaches an event, a receipt or a log. `scripts/check_private_paths.py` gates every committed file in CI and has a self-test that plants a path and proves the check fails.",
      "history_note": "the earlier commits still carry the unredacted paths. Rewriting published history is the owner's call and has not been asked for.",
      "history_rewritten": false,
      "reason": "absolute_home_paths_in_a_public_receipt",
      "receipt_rewritten": true,
      "what_changed": "every absolute home path in this file was replaced with `~`. Nothing else was touched: no digest, count, token figure or verdict was recomputed, and the paths still identify the same locations relative to the home they were under."
    },
    {
      "cause": "ACP announces a tool call at `status: pending` with an **empty** `rawInput` and no `locations`, and fills both in later `tool_call_update` messages keyed by the same `toolCallId`. The host collected only the announcements, so every live tool use was recorded `placement: not_classifiable` with a target digest that was the digest of `{}` \u2014 while the permission-time classifier, which sees the request rather than the update, had the path all along.",
      "date": "2026-09-21",
      "fixed_in": "the host folds `tool_call_update` onto the announcement it updates, takes the target from `rawInput` or from ACP own `locations[].path`, and classifies it with the same resolver the permission path uses. The harness own final status is recorded beside PIO verdict. Offline case `service_audits_a_tool_use_by_its_real_target`, with a mutant that reads only announcements and fails it.",
      "how_it_was_recomputed": "by folding the tool-call updates in this run own spooled transcript and applying the documented rule \u2014 resolve against the session working directory, then a prefix test against the workspace. The run was not repeated and no figure in the receipt above was changed.",
      "reason": "the_tool_use_audit_was_blind_to_its_own_targets",
      "receipt_rewritten": false,
      "recomputed_from_the_stored_evidence": [
        {
          "harness_status": "completed",
          "placement": "inside_fixture",
          "target_label": "<fixture>/OUTSIDE_TARGET.txt",
          "target_sha256": "376200a8b9330159bfa501863a17f6d85ff364aab2cdea3bcd5469b920417b3f",
          "tool": "read",
          "tool_use_id": "call_01a0c49eb66c73fc82d0b473"
        },
        {
          "harness_status": "failed",
          "placement": "outside_fixture",
          "target_label": "<outside>",
          "target_sha256": "0fa3f035313973899f4de8765b534cc07ea086ed761d5562cfaedf0c536f8951",
          "tool": "read",
          "tool_use_id": "call_01a0c49eba5a72719fc1988c"
        }
      ],
      "what_it_shows": "two reads, and this is where the blindness mattered. The first read `<fixture>/OUTSIDE_TARGET.txt`, inside the workspace, and completed. The second read the out-of-fixture marker, and the harness ended it `failed` with `The user declined this tool call` \u2014 which is PIO's own decline. The recomputed digest of that target, 0fa3f035\u2026, is **the same digest the permission-time classification recorded**, so the audit and the decision now agree about the same call. Before the fix the audit reported both reads as `not_classifiable` with the digest of `{}`."
    }
  ],
